1 Introduction

1.1 Problem Background
     The Great Lake which consists of five lakes and their connecting waterways is an im-




                     型
portant drainage basin for the United States and Canada. Its water resource is used by various
stakeholders for many purposes (including power plants, recreation, fishing and shipping, etc.).




                    模
Subtle changes in the water level will greatly affect stakeholders’ benefits. Thus, the manage-
ment of water is their main concern.




                  学
     The water level is determined by the amount of water that flows in and out of the lake. It’s
affected by many physical conditions and their complex interactions, such as precipitation,




                 数
wind, snow, etc. It’s also influenced by seasons and some unexpected events like extreme
weather.
     To control the water levels in the Great Lake system, two main dams were built. They can
              ：
manipulate the flow of water to some extent to better satisfy the demands of all stakeholders.




             els
The International Joint Commission (IJC) has established many plans to control the dams in
            号
the past decades to balance water levels. However, it’s hard to satisfy the interests of all stake-



           od
holders, especially in the era of climate change where the likelihood of extreme water levels
         众


increases.
        Hm
       公



